'''Grup Rojo, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|Grup Rojo}}), commonly known as '''Rojo''', is a Balboan [[petrochemicals|petrochemical]] company specialising in {{w|oxo alcohol}}s, {{w|plasticizer}}s, and {{w|polyethylene}}. Headquartered at 112 Carrer 5è, Zone 2, [[Sant Bart]], the company operates its main production complex at [[Badia Curta]] in [[Jala]] province, adjacent to [[CIEA]]'s refinery, which supplies naphtha feedstock. It is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] (SCX: GRO) and is a constituent of the [[SCX 100]] index.

Grup Rojo is the dominant domestic producer of oxo alcohols and plasticizers in Balboa, supplying the domestic {{w|PVC}} and plastics industries. The company also produces {{w|polyethylene}} (HDPE and LLDPE), in which it competes with [[Polímers de Balboa]] (PDB), the larger state-privatised plastics manufacturer. While PDB dominates the domestic polyethylene market, Grup Rojo has carved out a niche in specific grades and export markets.

== History ==

=== Founding (1968) ===

Grup Rojo was founded in 1968 by [[Pere Rojo i Vidal]], a Volisanian-Balboan chemical engineer who had worked at [[CIEA]]'s (then Petrolí de Balboa's) [[Badia Curta]] refinery since the late 1950s. Rojo identified an opportunity in the refinery's propylene output, which was being exported as raw material rather than processed into higher-value products. Using the {{w|oxo process}} ({{w|hydroformylation}}), propylene could be converted into {{w|oxo alcohol}}s — primarily {{w|2-ethylhexanol}} and {{w|n-butanol}} — which were in growing demand as feedstocks for plasticizer production.

Rojo left CIEA in 1967 and secured backing from a group of Sant Cristòfor private investors. He negotiated a long-term propylene supply agreement with Petrolí de Balboa and built the company's first oxo alcohol plant at Badia Curta in 1968. The company was named "Grup Rojo" after its founder.

=== Expansion into plasticizers (1970s) ===

In the early 1970s, Grup Rojo expanded downstream into {{w|plasticizer}} production, using its own oxo alcohols as feedstock. The company began producing {{w|DEHP}} (di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate) and {{w|DINP}} (diisononyl phthalate) for the domestic PVC industry, which was growing rapidly as the Balboan construction sector expanded. The vertical integration from propylene to oxo alcohols to plasticizers gave Grup Rojo a significant cost advantage over import-dependent competitors.

By the late 1970s, Grup Rojo was the dominant domestic supplier of oxo alcohols and plasticizers, supplying both the domestic market and exporting to Illypnian chemical companies. The establishment of the [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]] in 1981 further enhanced the company's export competitiveness.

=== Steam cracker and polyethylene (1980s) ===

In the early 1980s, Grup Rojo made a strategic decision to build its own {{w|steam cracker}} at Badia Curta, using {{w|naphtha}} supplied from CIEA's refinery. The steam cracker produced {{w|ethylene}} and {{w|propylene}}, giving the company an independent source of olefin feedstocks rather than relying entirely on CIEA's refinery byproducts. The ethylene was used to enter {{w|polyethylene}} production — specifically {{w|high-density polyethylene}} (HDPE) and {{w|linear low-density polyethylene}} (LLDPE).

The move into polyethylene brought Grup Rojo into direct competition with [[Polímers de Balboa]] (then Resines Nacionals de Balboa, RNB), the state-owned plastics manufacturer that had been established in 1979. While RNB dominated the domestic polyethylene market with a broader product range and larger capacity, Grup Rojo focused on specific HDPE and LLDPE grades for export markets, leveraging the negative tariff to compete on price.

=== Listing and growth (1992–present) ===

Grup Rojo was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1992. The listing raised capital that was used to expand the steam cracker capacity and modernise the oxo alcohol and plasticizer plants. The [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB) acquired a 6 per cent stake at the time of the listing, and CIEA acquired an 8 per cent strategic stake to formalise the long-term feedstock supply relationship.

Through the 1990s and 2000s, the company expanded its plasticizer portfolio, adding {{w|DOTP}} (di(2-ethylhexyl) terephthalate) and other non-phthalate plasticizers in response to growing regulatory restrictions on phthalate plasticizers in Illypnian markets. The company also invested in {{w|adipate}} plasticizers for low-temperature applications.

In the 2010s, Grup Rojo expanded its polyethylene capacity, targeting export markets in [[BTPMEU|BTPMEU countries]] and [[Illypnia]]. The company positioned itself as a lower-cost alternative to Illypnian polyethylene producers, benefiting from cheap naphtha feedstock  and the negative tariff on exports. While PDB remained the dominant polyethylene producer in Balboa, Grup Rojo's polyethylene business grew to account for approximately 25 per cent of revenue.

== Products ==

Grup Rojo's product portfolio spans three main categories:

=== Oxo alcohols ===

* '''2-ethylhexanol (2-EH)''': The company's primary oxo alcohol product, used as a feedstock for DEHP plasticizer production and sold to external customers for coatings, lubricants, and other applications.
* '''Isononanol (INA)''': Used as a feedstock for DINP plasticizer production.
* '''n-butanol and isobutanol''': Produced as co-products of the oxo process, sold as solvents and chemical intermediates.

=== Plasticizers ===

* '''DEHP (di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate)''': The company's traditional plasticizer product, used in flexible PVC for construction, automotive, and consumer goods. Production has declined in recent years due to regulatory restrictions in export markets.
* '''DINP (diisononyl phthalate)''': A general-purpose plasticizer for PVC, used in wire and cable insulation, flooring, and automotive interiors.
* '''DOTP (di(2-ethylhexyl) terephthalate)''': A non-phthalate plasticizer produced in response to regulatory restrictions on phthalates. Used in medical devices, toys, and food-contact applications. Grup Rojo is the largest domestic producer of DOTP.
* '''Adipate plasticizers''': Specialty plasticizers for low-temperature applications, used in automotive and aerospace industries.

=== Polyethylene ===

* '''HDPE (high-density polyethylene)''': Used in blow-moulded containers, injection-moulded products, and pipe. Grup Rojo's HDPE competes with PDB's product in both domestic and export markets.
* '''LLDPE (linear low-density polyethylene)''': Used in film, packaging, and injection-moulding applications. Grup Rojo focuses on specific LLDPE grades for export markets.

== Operations ==

* '''Headquarters''': 112 Carrer 5è, Zone 2, [[Sant Bart]]
* '''Main production complex''': [[Badia Curta]], [[Jala]] province — oxo alcohol plant, plasticizer plant, steam cracker, and polyethylene plant, all co-located with [[CIEA]]'s refinery
* '''Steam cracker''': [[Badia Curta]] — cracks naphtha (supplied from CIEA's refinery) into ethylene and propylene
* '''Export terminal''': [[Port de Portnou]] — handles export shipments of oxo alcohols, plasticizers, and polyethylene
* '''Feedstocks''': Naphtha from CIEA refinery; propylene from own steam cracker and from CIEA refinery
* '''Employees''': approximately 4,500 (2026)

The Badia Curta complex is Grup Rojo's sole production site, housing the steam cracker, oxo alcohol synthesis units, plasticizer plants, and polyethylene polymerisation reactors. The site's co-location with CIEA's refinery provides a significant cost advantage, as naphtha feedstock is delivered via direct pipeline rather than purchased on the open market.

== Ownership ==

Grup Rojo is publicly traded on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]]. The ownership structure reflects the company's history as a family-founded business with strategic institutional investors:

* '''[[Rojo family]]''': 15% — descendants of founder Pere Rojo i Vidal; represented on the board
* '''[[CIEA]]''': 8% — strategic stake tied to the long-term naphtha supply agreement
* '''[[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB)''': 6% — acquired at the time of the 1992 listing
* '''[[Balboa Pension Fund]] (BPF)''': 4% — institutional anchor investor
* '''Public float''': approximately 67% — held by institutional and retail investors

== Competition ==

Grup Rojo competes directly with [[Polímers de Balboa]] (PDB) in the polyethylene market. While PDB is the dominant polyethylene producer in Balboa — with a broader product range including polypropylene, PVC, and PET — Grup Rojo has established a niche in specific HDPE and LLDPE grades, particularly for export markets. The two companies share the Badia Curta chemical complex and both depend on CIEA for feedstock, creating a complex competitive dynamic.

In the oxo alcohol and plasticizer markets, Grup Rojo faces limited domestic competition, as these products are not part of PDB's portfolio. The company's main competitors in these markets are international chemical companies, against which Grup Rojo competes on cost — benefiting from cheap naphtha feedstock, low hydropower costs, and the negative tariff on exports.

